Chapter 1: The Girl from Madhavpur

In the quiet lanes of Madhavpur, a small town nestled among golden fields and peaceful hills, lived a girl named Anu Sharma. Her dreams stretched far beyond the village’s borders. At 22, Anu wasn’t just another daughter in a traditional household—she was a storm of ambition wrapped in calm determination.

Anu belonged to a modest middle-class family. Her father, Ramesh Sharma, was a hardworking farmer who spent his days under the sun, cultivating the paddy fields that had sustained generations. Her mother, Savita Sharma, was a devoted housewife who ran the home with discipline and care.

Despite her academic brilliance, Anu never shied away from responsibility. Every morning, before heading to college or opening her books, she helped her mother in the kitchen—cutting vegetables, lighting the stove, kneading the dough. During harvest season, she tied her dupatta tight and stepped into the paddy fields beside her father, working the land with hands that also turned pages of poetry and prose.

